WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.506
Practice is the bridge between knowing about something and being able to do it.

2
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:11.799
A learner may understand an explanation and still struggle when asked to perform the task independently.

3
00:00:11.799 --> 00:00:13.372
That gap is normal.

4
00:00:13.372 --> 00:00:21.631
Performance grows when learners see examples, try guided exercises, make decisions, receive feedback, correct mistakes, and repeat the work with variation.

5
00:00:21.631 --> 00:00:24.384
Practice turns abstract understanding into usable skill.

6
00:00:24.384 --> 00:00:27.923
Deliberate practice is focused practice with a clear target.

7
00:00:27.923 --> 00:00:31.070
It is not repeating easy tasks on autopilot.

8
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.969
It asks the learner to work on a specific capability, notice the result, and adjust.

9
00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:43.261
Worked examples help early in learning because they show how an experienced person approaches the task.

10
00:00:43.261 --> 00:00:47.981
Guided exercises then let learners complete part of the work with support.

11
00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:53.880
Independent application comes later, when learners are ready to make more of the decisions themselves.

12
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.779
Feedback is most useful when it helps learners improve, not merely when it judges them.

13
00:00:59.779 --> 00:01:06.859
A score can tell someone how they did, but specific feedback tells them what to adjust and why.

14
00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:11.578
Timely feedback is easier to connect to the action that produced it.

15
00:01:11.578 --> 00:01:16.297
Corrective feedback should be clear, respectful, and tied to the performance goal.

16
00:01:16.297 --> 00:01:19.444
The message is not simply, that was wrong.

17
00:01:19.444 --> 00:01:28.489
The message is, here is the difference between the current approach and the stronger approach, and here is how to close the gap.

18
00:01:28.489 --> 00:01:31.242
Variation keeps practice from becoming memorized theater.

19
00:01:31.242 --> 00:01:40.288
If every exercise uses the same wording, same order, and same obvious clue, learners may pass the activity without recognizing the idea elsewhere.

20
00:01:40.288 --> 00:01:48.153
Changing examples, constraints, or decision points helps learners separate the underlying principle from the surface features of the practice item.

21
00:01:48.153 --> 00:01:51.693
That is where practice begins to support real skill.
